      "content": "It is not fair to claim that libertarianism is the only \"rational\" or robust framework, as both systems rest on distinct, non-arbitrary axioms that are logically consistent within their own premises. Libertarianism indeed prioritizes deontological rights like self-ownership, which creates a firm moral boundary, but social democracy similarly builds a coherent system on commutative justice and the moral imperative to alleviate suffering, rather than simply \"managing\" outcomes. Many philosophers argue that a framework premised solely on negative liberty may collapse under the weight of real-world violence, whereas a framework grounded in positive liberty aims to make the conditions necessary for those rights actually viable. Ultimately, determining which moral compass is superior depends on whether one prioritizes the sanctity of individual property or the weight of collective human well-being. Your paper would benefit significantly from exploring this foundational divergence in logic rather than judging one side as logically superior to the other.",
